6. Collision
-------------
This is one of the most important topics in Unit 2.

A Collision occurs when two or more different keys produce the same hash table index.

Let's understand with an example.

Hash function:


H(key)=key mod 10

Suppose:


    H(57)=57\bmod10=7


    H(77)=77\bmod10=7


Both keys want to occupy index 7.

But one position cannot directly hold both values in a normal single-slot hash table.

This situation is called Collision.



======================================================================================================================================


7. Synonyms
------------
When two different keys produce the same hash address, those keys are called Synonyms.

Example:

H(57)=7



H(77)=7


Here, 57 and 77 are synonyms.

Difference:
- Collision = The situation/problem.
- Synonyms = The different keys that cause that collision.



======================================================================================================================================


8. Overflow
------------
Overflow occurs when there is no empty space available in the hash table to insert a new record.

Example:
--------

Suppose index 7 already contains 57.

Now we want to insert 77.

If index 7 is occupied and all the positions we try next are also occupied, we cannot insert 77.

This situation is called Overflow.

Remember the difference:
-------------------------

Collision	                                              Overflow
Two keys get the same index.	                      No suitable empty space is available.
Happens because of the same hash address.	          Happens when insertion cannot find space.





